## CK Architecture Note — Scene 2 as Multiple Scenes (IMPLEMENTED BELOW)

Scene 2 is split into multiple scenes gated on `Scene.Start()` topic info fragments from the Force Greet, combined with Stability/Affinity global variable checks for path selection.

**Why:** Scene 2 has two independent axes of variation — the player's Force Greet choice (5 options) and Alenawe's path (A/B/C). Putting both into one scene means every dialogue phase needs compound conditions. Splitting into separate scenes gives each path a clean, linear dialogue sequence that's individually testable and can breathe at its own rhythm. Path A's negotiation is procedural and efficient. Path C has more back-and-forth. Path B has longer silences and fewer words. Separate scenes let each path own its pacing, phase count, and emotional register instead of forcing them into identical beat structures.

**Proposed structure:**

| Scene | Gating | Content |
|-------|--------|---------|
| Scene 2A | Path A conditions (high Stability, medium Affinity) | Opening variant → Path A argument (field reclassification) |
| Scene 2B | Path B conditions (high Affinity, lower Stability) | Opening variant → Path B argument (outgrown the framework) |
| Scene 2C | Path C conditions (high Stability + higher Affinity) | Opening variant → Path C argument (network proposal) |
| Scene 2X | Force Greet 5 override (indifferent — extraction timeline stands) | Truncated/desperate variant — she's fighting from a deficit with no one in her corner |
| Scene 3 | Universal — fires after whichever Scene 2 completes | Beat 7 (Alonythil's fracture reveal) → Beat 8 (the closing) |

**Key implementation details:**

- Each Scene 2 variant contains its OWN opening exchange (the "Room After" material currently written as conditional blocks below). The Force Greet topic info fragment determines which opening lines fire within each path scene. This means 2-3 conditional lines per scene instead of 15+ in a monolith.
- Scene 2X (Force Greet 5 / indifferent) overrides path selection entirely. When the player says "take her, it doesn't matter," the extraction timeline is back on the table regardless of Stability/Affinity. This scene can be structurally different — shorter, more desperate, feeds into Shadow Path if she can't recover.
- Beats 7 and 8 (Alonythil's fracture and the closing) are universal — they fire the same regardless of path. These go in Scene 3, which triggers after any Scene 2 variant completes.
- Alenawe's Scene 1 refusal variants (the "prepared remarks") remain conditional phases within Scene 1, gated on Stability/Affinity globals. That's one fork point in one scene — manageable.
- Beat 5 (the old "System's Logic" convergence beat) dissolves. Each path scene handles its own transition from the Opening into the argument at its own pace. Path A might have Alonythil give a thin "walk me through it." Path B might have her start talking before he's settled. Path C might have a brief institutional framing line she immediately redirects. The convergence point was only a structural problem because one scene was trying to serve three emotional registers.

**For VO recording:** Each scene is a continuous emotional arc. The actors can stay in register instead of jumping between conditional contexts. Path B Alenawe and Path A Alenawe are in fundamentally different emotional spaces — separate sessions, separate scenes.

*The content below implements the scene architecture defined above. Each Scene 2 variant is a self-contained NPC-to-NPC scene with its own opening exchange and argument. Scene 3 is universal.*

