## Why This Frame is Stronger (Design Notes)

**She argues against success, not suspicion.**
The old frame puts her on trial — "explain the discrepancy." The new frame offers her an exit — "you've done well, come home." Refusing a reward is more revealing than surviving an interrogation. You don't turn down institutional recognition unless something has fundamentally shifted inside you.

**She's not defending — she's PROPOSING.**
Each path has her pitching a different future, not arguing against extraction. Path A requests a role that already exists (field operative). Path C proposes something that doesn't exist yet (a network). Path B rejects the entire taxonomy. The institution can process A. It can evaluate C. It cannot categorize B. That gradient — from fillable to unclassifiable — IS the arc.

**Alonythil is more dangerous distracted.**
As personal handler, his full attention on her case says "you matter." As regional coordinator, his partial attention says "you are one of many." The institutional intimacy is gone. When he DOES focus — when she says something that catches him — that attention weighs more because it's chosen, not obligated. And the detail that she's the stop he lingered on longest, that neither of them will name — that's where his humanity lives.

**The Alinor posting becomes ironic because he held it.**
He's offering her the exact position he left. He knows what it does to someone like her — quiet, precise, slowly hollowing. But now it's worse than hollowing: the Alinor desk is where she'd find the answers. The destabilization posture, the strategic logic, the full picture of what the Dominion is doing in Skyrim and why. He found those answers. They made the machinery visible. He offered her the desk anyway because the system says it's correct — and because the alternative is leaving her in a field where her effective work runs counter to the strategic posture, next to a kill-on-sight designee who she can't stop helping. His fracture moment ("I found the answers / they were not useful") is him describing the destination he just tried to send her to. He knows what she'd find there. He's either trying to spare her the field or spare her the desk. He can't do both.

**The reports become ironic.**
The Thalmor are confident because her reports were good. And the reports ARE good — accurate, clean, institutionally legible. The irony is double: the format filters out the operative (she's invisible inside the form she was trained to fill), and the content describes stabilization in a destabilization theater. She didn't compromise the data. She didn't need to. The institution's own framework can't model what she's describing, and the institution's own strategic posture contradicts what she's documenting. The analytical division reads her reports and sees a containable variable. Alonythil reads them and sees his best operative producing a detailed record of counter-mission work. The only person in the room who knows the model is wrong is the person who can't explain why without breaching strategic classification to a kill-on-sight designee standing three feet away.

**The gameplay justification is organic.**
She stays not because she won an argument, but because the situation is too volatile and her proposals too reasonable (or too unclassifiable) to deny outright. Path A: he files paperwork. Path C: he flags it for review. Path B: he defers because there's no form. In all three cases, the institution doesn't agree. It calculates — or it stalls. The leash gets longer. For now. That preserves tension for MajQ2, where the system comes back to check whether her calculation was worth it.

**The Force Greet is more interesting.**
Asks the player to be honest. What they say tells Alonythil — and Alenawe — what this relationship actually is. The player isn't defending a position. They're exposing one.
